      "math_academy_id": "q-332827",
      "sequence_position": 1,
      "problem": "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-2{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation, we get the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)-2={{field-1}} \\\\ {{field-2}}=-2{X}_{1}(s)+4{X}_{2}(s)+{{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}$ are the Laplace transforms of the functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t),$ respectively.",
      "worked_solution": "First, we compute the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}L\\{{x}_{1}^{′}(t)\\}=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\} \\\\ L\\{{x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\}=L\\{-2{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}sL\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}-{x}_{1}(0)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}-5L\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\} \\\\ sL\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}-{x}_{2}(0)=-2L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}+4L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}+2L\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the results\n\n$$\nL\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1},\\,L\\{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\}=\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1},\n$$\n\nwe can write our system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)-2={X}_{1}(s)-\\frac{5s}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)+1=-2{X}_{1}(s)+4{X}_{2}(s)+\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}$ are the Laplace transforms of the functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t),$ respectively.",
